- Mouse Text Selection: design notes and where we landed #decision
    - Last Updated: 2026-07-09
    - Context: several rounds of back-and-forth trying to make tmux mouse selection
      feel like a normal terminal. This note records what we tried, why it did not
      work, and the setup we settled on, so we do not relitigate it later.

- The goal we kept chasing
    - Drag-select text with the mouse and have it behave like a bare terminal
        - Selection stays highlighted after the button is released
        - No jump to the bottom of the scrollback on release
        - No modal "copy mode" trap: you can just keep typing afterwards
        - Selecting auto-copies to the system clipboard
    - Also keep the useful tmux mouse features
        - Wheel scroll into scrollback
        - Click a pane to focus it
        - Drag a border to resize

- The fundamental constraint (the thing to remember) #decision #important
    - tmux mouse selection is inherently modal: it IS copy-mode. There is no
      non-modal "select like a terminal" mode. Long-standing tmux feature request
      that was never implemented (tmux #732).
    - The Shift-to-bypass is NOT a tmux binding we can change. It is hardcoded in
      VTE, the engine behind mate-terminal. VTE exposes no setting to invert or
      disable it. Holding Shift toggles the mouse between "terminal mode" and
      "application mode" (ghostty discussion #5800, tmux-users list).
    - Wheel scroll and drag-capture are ONE switch, not two. tmux only receives
      wheel events while mouse reporting is on, and while mouse reporting is on VTE
      refuses native selection unless Shift is held. You cannot tell tmux "report
      the wheel to me but let drags fall through." (tmux #2283, tmux #3200, both
      closed with no such feature.)
    - Net: within tmux + mate-terminal you cannot have plain-drag native selection
      AND wheel scroll at the same time. Pick one, hold Shift, or change terminal.

- What we tried in copy-mode and why each fell short
    - copy-pipe-and-cancel on drag end (this was the tmux default)
        - The "-and-cancel" exits copy mode, which clears the highlight and snaps
          the view to the bottom. This is the "escape on release" annoyance.
    - copy-pipe-no-clear on drag end
        - Copies to clipboard, keeps the highlight, stays in copy mode. Fixes the
          jump and the vanishing highlight.
        - But the selection stays "live", so scrolling afterwards keeps EXTENDING
          the selection.
    - copy-pipe-no-clear + stop-selection on drag end #decision
        - stop-selection finalizes the selection so it is no longer live. Scrolling
          afterwards moves the view without growing the selection. This is the
          current plain-drag behavior.
        - Remaining unavoidable trade-off: you are still in copy-mode after
          selecting, so keystrokes go to copy-mode until you press q or Escape.
    - MouseDown / MouseUp clear-and-exit hacks (earlier attempt, abandoned)
        - Starting a drag from scrollback jumped to the bottom (mouse-down fires at
          drag start). Clicking after a selection jumped to the bottom.
        - Each fix exposed a new edge case. Concluded these hacks are a dead end.

- Options that WOULD give true plain-drag native selection, and why we did not pick them
    - Turn tmux mouse off entirely (set -g mouse off)
        - Plain drag becomes true native terminal selection with copy-on-select.
        - Cost: loses wheel scroll, click-to-focus, and drag-resize. We need wheel
          scroll, so this is out.
    - Switch terminal to kitty or WezTerm (both FOSS)
        - kitty mouse_map has explicit grabbed/ungrabbed states, so plain left-drag
          can select even while tmux grabs the mouse, and the wheel still reaches
          tmux. This is the ONLY way to get the real swap plus keep tmux scroll.
        - Not chosen for now: we decided to stay on mate-terminal.

- Where we landed #decision
    - Keep set -g mouse on. Two selection modes coexist:
        - Plain drag = tmux copy-mode selection. On release it copies to the
          clipboard via xclip, keeps the highlight, and does not jump. Bindings:
          MouseDragEnd1Pane -> copy-pipe-no-clear "xclip -selection clipboard -in"
          then stop-selection, in both copy-mode and copy-mode-vi.
        - Shift+drag = mate-terminal native selection (bypasses tmux). Highlight
          persists, no copy mode, type immediately. Limited to visible text, not
          scrollback. Enable "copy on select" in mate-terminal, or Ctrl+Shift+C.
    - Keyboard copy workflow: prefix [ to enter copy mode, move, select, then
      Enter or M-w to copy to the clipboard.
    - The exact swap the user wanted (plain drag native, Shift+drag = tmux) is not
      achievable on mate-terminal. Revisit only by moving to kitty or WezTerm.

- References
    - tmux #732  select in normal mode without copy-mode (open, never done)
    - tmux #2283 disable drag select but keep scroll (closed, no feature)
    - tmux #3200 scroll without interfering with selecting (closed, no feature)
    - tmux #2550 disable copy mode while scrolling
    - ghostty discussion #5800  VTE Shift override is hardcoded, not configurable
    - odetarou writeup on keeping scroll position with stop-selection
    - Config lives in conf/tmux.conf under the "Copy Mode" section
